 "section_summary": "This section concerns Roy Hoko Ikeda's efforts in 1949 to establish an anti-communist research institute in Japan and his solicitations for support. It contains Ikeda's letter to George F. Kennan with an attached list of prominent Japanese contacts and a calling card, seeking funding from the Carnegie Endowment for International Peace. It also includes State Department security memoranda and an instruction to the U.S. Political Adviser in Tokyo reviewing Ikeda's prewar communist activities, wartime internment at Topaz, and denial of an exit permit in 1947.",
